{
    "title": "Identification (ID) Cards for Members of the Uniformed Services, Their Dependents, and Other Eligible Individuals; Amendment",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 01, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-01/pdf/2025-21723.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register delivers three notable updates: a fee cut from the Treasury and IRS for an estate-related service, an immediate change by the Department of Defense to how sex is recorded in military ID systems, and a small inflation adjustment to music royalty rates for physical and download formats in 2026.\n\nFirst, the Treasury and IRS finalized a previously proposed user fee reduction tied to a specific estate service, setting the fee at $56. The agencies say this won\u2019t meaningfully affect small businesses and is not economically significant. Executors and families handling estates should see a modest cost savings. There\u2019s little apparent downside beyond a slight decrease in fee revenue for the government, and the rule leaves broader tax issues untouched.\n\nSecond, the Department of Defense issued an interim final rule, effective immediately, changing how personal data is handled for retirees, dependents, and contractor employees in its eligibility and ID systems. The rule replaces references to \u201cgender\u201d with \u201csex\u201d and removes the option for these groups to request changes to that data, unless there was an administrative error. The department cites clarity and consistency with a January 2025 executive order and is seeking public comments through January 30, 2026. Supporters of fixed sex classification and streamlined administration may view this as a win for uniform records. Those negatively affected are likely transgender and nonbinary individuals who could previously update this information, and who may now face mismatches between military records and other identification or personal documents.\n\nLastly, the Copyright Royalty Board announced a routine cost\u2011of\u2011living increase for the statutory license covering physical records and permanent downloads of songs for 2026. The per\u2011song rate moves to 13.1 cents and the per\u2011minute rate to 2.52 cents. Songwriters and music publishers benefit from a modest boost aligned with inflation. Music services that sell downloads, record labels, and potentially consumers could see slightly higher costs, though streaming services are not affected by this update.\n\nEach change is relatively narrow, but together they touch taxes, military identification policy, and the business of music\u2014areas that matter to families settling estates, military communities, and the creative economy.",
